  "slug": "ai-agents-biological-4390",
  "summary": "Generative and predictive drug models are increasingly tested against PBPK exposure, measured resistance and transporter risk endpoints.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is evaluating computational predictions against downstream criteria rather than a single predicted score. Exposure-aware evaluation re-scores PARP1 generated molecules with PBPK simulations, showing that dose-driven exposure and safety limits reorder which candidates look promising. HEDGEHOG applies a six-stage workflow to 22 generative models on KRAS G12D and reveals stage-wise attrition that standard metrics miss. Leave-one-out cross-validated classifiers predict artemisinin resistance in P. falciparum K13 mutants from structure-derived physicochemical features, while classic and graph-based QSAR models trained on P-gp, BCRP and MRP1 inhibition data target ABC transporter drug-drug interaction risk. What is new is the explicit move of validation toward pharmacokinetic, resistance and transport endpoints rather than ranking on predicted activity alone. The items are heterogeneous in task, so this describes a dominant methodological trend rather than a uniform finding.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-11"
}
